{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Jacob's firstborn, Reuben.</b> In this very place where his sin is mentioned, he is called by the name 'firstborn,' even though the birthright was taken from him as punishment for this. This comes to teach that the birthright that was taken from him and given to Joseph was the birthright of property or the birthright of honor, that his sons would be called tribes, but the birthright of lineage surely remained his, for who could take it from him? And this is what it says: <b>Jacob's firstborn, Reuben</b> — this is the birthright of lineage, that he was Jacob's firstborn. And so you find in Bereshit Rabbah: <b>And it came to pass, when Israel dwelt</b> — R. Shimon said: it is difficult before the Holy One, blessed be He, to uproot a chain of lineage from its place; as it is written (I Chronicles 5:1): ״ובני ראובן בכור ישראל כי הוא הבכור ובחללו יצועי אביו נתנה בכורתו לבני יוסף בן ישראל ולא להתיחש לבכורה״ — say now: the birthright of property was taken from him, but the birthright of lineage was not taken from him. Rabbi Yitzḥak says: even at the time of the sin, they trace lineage only to Reuben, as it is written: <b>And it came to pass, when Israel dwelt</b> etc., <b>Jacob's firstborn, Reuben.</b> Rabbi Yudan said: firstborn for service, firstborn for birth, firstborn for repentance. R. Azariah says: also firstborn for prophecy, as it is said (Hosea 1:2): ״תחלת דבר ה׳ בהושע״ — end of quotation."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:57.062440669Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}